text:
This is a first-in-human (FIH), phase 1, multicenter, open-label, dose-escalation study
of SKB518 to evaluate the safety, tolerability, PK, immunogenicity, and antitumor
activity in adult subjects with advanced or metastatic solid tumor relapsed/refractory to
standard therapies or for which no effective standard therapy is available.

Inclusion Criteria:
- Subject must be at least 18 years of age at the time of signing the informed consent;
- Histological or cytological diagnosis of advanced/metastatic solid tumor that has progressed on standard treatments;
- Ability to provide tumor blocks or slides for biomarkers testing;
- At least one measurable lesion per RECIST v1.1;
- ECOG performance status score of 0 or 1;
- Life expectancy of at least 3 months;
- Adequate organ and bone marrow function;
- Recovery from all toxicities from previous therapy;
- Effective contraception for subjects of childbearing potential;
- Ability to provide voluntary informed consent.

Exclusion Criteria:
- Active or untreated CNS metastases or carcinomatous meningitis;
- Additional malignancy requiring treatment within the past 5 years;
- Major cardiovascular, cerebrovascular, or thromboembolic diseases;
- Serious/uncontrolled concomitant diseases;
- Active tuberculosis;
- Uncontrolled HIV infection;
- Active viral hepatitis;
- Major surgery within 28 days prior to the first dose;
- Allergy to SKB518 or its excipients;
- History of ILD or non-infectious pneumonitis requiring steroids;
- Severe lung injuries or severe dry eye syndrome;
- History of allogeneic transplant;
- Uncontrollable effusion;
- Live vaccine within 30 days before the first dose;
- Use of strong CYP3A4 or BCRP inhibitors within 2 weeks before the first dose;
- Recent chemotherapy, radiotherapy, or other specific treatments;
- Active infection requiring systemic therapy;
- Systemic corticosteroid or immunosuppressive therapy within 14 days before the first dose;
- Participation in another investigational study within 28 days of the first dose;
- Rapid deterioration of health before the first dose;
- Known psychiatric or substance abuse disorders;
- Other conditions that may interfere with the study.

